5, -0.0118865966796875, -0.0207672119140625, 0.00533294677734375, -0.0248260498046875, -0.014617919921875, 0.0191650390625, -0.0033435821533203125, -0.0011777877807617188, -0.008056640625, 0.0266265869140625, 0.01407623291015625, 0.01389312744140625, -0.04644775390625, -0.0116119384765625, -0.031005859375, 0.0019006729125976562, 0.00913238525390625, -0.00136566162109375, 0.01708984375, -0.0239105224609375, -0.006488800048828125, 0.00647735595703125, -0.017059326171875, 0.007843017578125, -0.035308837890625, -0.0338134765625, 0.00966644287109375, 0.031341552734375, -0.00882720947265625, -0.01140594482421875, 0.0197906494140625, -0.0201873779296875, 0.0110015869140625, -0.00853729248046875, -0.01959228515625, -0.0274658203125, 0.01641845703125, 0.01342010498046875, -0.021697998046875, 0.0033931732177734375, 0.01519012451171875, -0.01220703125, 0.03125, 0.003993988037109375, 0.0102996826171875, 0.025604248046875, -0.0097198486328125, 0.027313232421875, -0.006946563720703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. Here, we introduce a novel framework for robustly quantifying and distinguishing local spatial phase relationships in oscillator networks, particularly during nonstationary or partially synchronized states where traditional global or amplitude-based measures may fail. Unlike existing approaches, our method leverages spatial ordinal patterns to capture local phase structure, enabling sensitive detection of complex and transient spatiotemporal organization.\n\n**Methods**\n\nOur framework is based on spatial ordinal patterns that encode the local ordering of oscillator phases. For each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its immediate neighbors. Specifically, for a given oscillator \\(i\\) with phase \\(\\phi_i\\), we compute the difference with its left (\\(\\phi_{i-1}\\)) and right (\\(\\phi_{i+1}\\)) neighbors. If \\(|\\phi_i - \\phi_j| < \\epsilon\\) (where \\(j\\) is a neighbor and \\(\\epsilon\\) is a small threshold), we assign a tie symbol \u20180\u2019; if \\(\\phi_i > \\phi_j + \\epsilon\\), we assign \u2018+\u2019; if \\(\\phi_i < \\phi_j - \\epsilon\\), we assign \u2018\u2212\u2019. For example, if an oscillator\u2019s phase is higher than its left neighbor but equal (within \\(\\epsilon\\)) to its right neighbor, its pattern is encoded as (\u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019). This stepwise encoding ensures that near-equal phases are not misclassified due to noise or discretization. The threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) is chosen based on the expected phase noise or discretization error; in our experiments, we set \\(\\epsilon\\) to 5% of the full phase range, but we confirm that results are robust for \\(\\epsilon\\) between 2\u201310%.\n\nFrom these symbolic representations, we compute the spatial permutation entropy at each time point. This is done by calculating the frequency of each possible ordinal pattern across all oscillators in the network and then applying the Shannon entropy formula: \\(H = -\\sum_{k} p_k \\log p_k\\), where \\(p_k\\) is the probability of observing pattern \\(k\\). Low entropy indicates uniform phase ordering (e.g., global synchrony or clustering), while high entropy reflects complex or disordered arrangements. This measure directly quantifies the diversity of local phase configurations, providing a sensitive indicator of spatiotemporal complexity.\n\n**Validation and Results**\n\nWe validate our approach on synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data. For synthetic data, we use networks of 100\u2013200 Kuramoto oscillators with ring and small-world topologies, varying coupling strengths and adding Gaussian phase noise (standard deviation up to 0.1 radians). We benchmark against standard global order parameters and amplitude-based entropy. Our method distinguishes between phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but different spatial arrangements, and sensitively detects transitions between chimera and fully synchronized states. Quantitatively, spatial permutation entropy achieves higher sensitivity (AUC > 0.9, Wilcoxon rank-sum test, p < 0.001) in detecting these transitions compared to global order parameters (AUC \u2248 0.7) and amplitude-based entropy (AUC \u2248 0.6).\n\nFor EEG data, we analyze resting-state recordings from 12 healthy volunteers (10-minute sessions, 64 channels, standard preprocessing including bandpass filtering and artifact rejection). We extract instantaneous phases using the Hilbert transform and compute spatial ordinal patterns across neighboring electrodes. Our method differentiates between eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions within individuals (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.2, paired t-test, p < 0.01), outperforming amplitude-based entropy (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.7). Full details of preprocessing and statistical evaluation are provided in the Supplement.\n\n**Discussion**\n\nOur approach is robust to moderate noise and does not require regular network topology, though extreme noise or highly irregular connectivity may reduce sensitivity. The choice of \\(\\epsilon\\) can affect sensitivity to small phase differences; we recommend calibrating \\(\\epsilon\\) based on noise estimates or using cross-validation. The method assumes reliable phase estimation and may be less effective in networks with very high noise, rapidly changing topology, or where phase is ill-defined. In such cases, amplitude-based or alternative measures may be preferable. \n\nIn summary, spatial ordinal pattern analysis provides a powerful and adaptable tool for uncovering and quantifying the rich spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems, highlighting the importance of local phase structure beyond global synchronization. Our results demonstrate its utility across both synthetic and real-world data, with clear advantages over traditional global and amplitude-based measures in sensitivity to spatial phase organization."}}